waymo,2026-06,18827345,277829761,275179379,281379379,17091585,21013105,1,1,1,1,1,1,"US est., bottom-up on the Waymo hub's MEASURED Q2 (Sep 24, 2026 update: 271.329M rider-only miles thru Jun-2026; re-chained 2026-09-25): CA = CPUC Q2-2026 driverless actual 9,023,791 deployment + 660,877 pilot (CPUC's total driverless VMT incl. deadhead) x 1.0576, the hub-vs-CPUC ratio for the hub's CA counties in Q2 (Q1: 1.040) = 10.24M (±3%); Maricopa 3.78M and Travis 1.84M = the hub's measured Q2 county increments (11.57M / 5.28M) split by the prior monthly shape (±10%); Atlanta 1.17M = the hub's measured Q2 Fulton+DeKalb increment (3.25M) split by the prior monthly shape (±10%); + rider-only metros the hub does not count (Miami, Dallas, Houston, Orlando, Nashville; San Antonio passenger service halted Apr 20 to Sep 17 after flood strandings Apr 4 and Apr 20) 1.80M [1.05M, 3.00M]. Public freeway rides suspended all month (Waymo's recall filing 26E035000: 'Waymo resumed vehicle operations on freeway June 25, 2026'); Nashville open to all Jun 25; the CPUC pilot file carries the Ojai (Zeekr) trusted-tester rides. The Jun-2026 cumulative lands on the anchor: the hub's 271.329M plus 6.65M estimated in metros the hub does not count, less ~0.15M pre-series miles; its cumulative band is the hub total plus 4.0M to 10.2M for those metros, less 0.15M (data/README.md, Waymo anchors). An Atlanta double-count correction applied 2026-09-25 was withdrawn 2026-09-29: Waymo's corrected per-cell file (CSV4 v2, posted Sep 28 but, as of Oct 2, not yet linked from waymo.com/safety/impact) lists each cell once and sums to the published county totals."
zoox,2026-06,265000,2745920,2230213,3251673,185500,344500,1,1,1,1,1,1,"US rough est.: LV Strip service (Zoox app; Uber partnership announced Mar 2026, LV Uber-app launch slated summer 2026) + SF east-side expansion (spring 2026), Austin/Miami employee rides; still free — NHTSA commercial FMVSS exemption granted Jul 30, 2026 (paid LV rides begin Aug); 0.7x-1.3x band. CPUC Q2-2026 (posted Aug 18, 2026) CA driverless actual: 67,923 mi this month (SF only; ~26% of the estimate) — too small a share to narrow the 0.7x-1.3x band, which stays as authored."
tesla,2026-07,279000,2719000,2612800,3126000,246000,320000,1,1,1,1,1,1,"Tesla stopped charting miles from every mode after Q2: its Q3-2026 update deck (p8, 'Cumulative Unsupervised Robotaxi Miles') and its Robotaxi Safety Report page (tesla.com/robotaxi/safety, live Oct 21, 2026, which embeds weekly cumulative miles per city) count only driving with no safety monitor in the vehicle, including remote-operator assistance or takeover; Tesla does not say whether repositioning or employee rides are in them. That is a subset of the scope here (every Tesla operator mode, monitor aboard or not), so it sets the floor: July's no-monitor miles are 246,410 on the page (city sums interpolated to Jul 31 within its week) and 251,469 in the deck; floor 246,000. The central adds 33,000 monitor-aboard miles, June's measured remainder (the Q2 deck's 157,000 paid miles less the page's 123,823 no-monitor miles), held flat; the top, 320,000, assumes the monitor-aboard share stayed at or below June's 21% (the higher reading x 1.27). The page's series reproduces Tesla's 'more than 380,000' unsupervised miles of Jul 22 (388,672 by that date). July launches: Miami driverless Jul 3, Tampa and Orlando Jul 21."
waymo,2026-07,19477348,297307109,290893979,305162839,15714600,23783460,1,1,1,1,1,1,"US est., bottom-up extension on the hub basis (re-chained 2026-09-25): CA = June-trend CPUC-basis extrapolation 9.85M (±10%) x the hub-vs-CPUC CA ratio 1.058 [1.040, 1.076] = 10.42M; Maricopa 3.95M (hub Q2 run-rate 3.86M/mo; public freeway rides back Jul 29 from Phoenix; Waymo's recall filing 26E035000 says freeway vehicle operations resumed Jun 25), Travis 1.85M, Atlanta 1.26M (hub Q2 run-rate 1.08M/mo) (±25%); + uncounted metros 2.0M [1.2M, 3.3M] (12 JUL SGO crashes in them ≈ 1.8M at the 6.6/M young-market crash rate; Denver/Las Vegas/San Diego/Tampa employee rider-only from ~Jul 8). SF ~1-hour pause Jul 18 (power outage); LA/SF Bay freeways back ~first week of Aug. No Waymo total-miles figure measured after Jun 2026 exists; later figures are floors: 'more than 4,000' vehicles (TechCrunch, Sep 1; it already said 'around 4,000' on Jun 29; the Jun 13 recall covered 3,871 5th-gen vehicles) and 'more than half a million paid trips weekly' (Waymo, Sep 15, 2026; the same floor Waymo has stated since late March), while '4M+ mi/wk' was last stated Jun 24. Next anchors: CPUC Q3 (~mid-Nov) and the hub update with data thru Sep (~mid/late Dec)."
zoox,2026-07,265000,3010920,2415713,3596173,185500,344500,1,1,1,1,1,1,"US rough est.: LV Strip service + SF (east-side expansion), Austin/Miami employee rides, all still free through July; NHTSA granted the FMVSS commercial exemption Jul 30, 2026 (first ever for a no-controls robotaxi; 8 standards, up to 2,500 vehicles/yr for 2 years) — paid LV rides begin Aug 10, 2026 (announced Aug 5); 0.7x-1.3x band. Cume matches Zoox's 'more than 3 million miles on public roads' (company statement of Aug 5, 2026, CNBC/Bloomberg; not on zoox.com). NTA's Apr 6, 2026 interim authority under permit AVNC 001 capped the Las Vegas fleet at 100 vehicles (the posted Sub 5 is dated Aug 28), with ~65 running at the Aug 10 paid launch; purpose-built fleet 105 per smoke recall 26E044000 (received Jul 10); no service pause Jul-Aug."
tesla,2026-08,517000,3236000,3079800,3741000,467000,615000,1,1,1,1,1,1,"Second month on Tesla's no-monitor miles (method in the 2026-07 row). August: 483,680 on the page, 467,685 in the deck; floor 467,000. The central, 517,000, adds June's 33,000 monitor-aboard remainder: monitor-aboard driving continued (NHTSA report 13781-16672, an Austin crash in August with 'a safety monitor present in the vehicle', is the only Tesla report since May 23 that Tesla's own no-monitor list leaves out); the top, 615,000, keeps the monitor-aboard share at or below June's 21%. The page's series reproduces Tesla's Sep-3 '1 million miles of unsupervised Robotaxi operation' (1,035,164 by Sep 3). The cumulative band's floor through Aug 31, 3,079,800, is the end-June floor (2,366,800: the deck's 2.44M less ~3% read noise) plus the July and August floors."
waymo,2026-08,20759027,318066136,307407059,330821087,16513080,25658248,1,1,1,1,1,1,"US est., bottom-up extension on the hub basis (re-chained 2026-09-25): CA = 10.4M CPUC-basis (±12%; freeways back early Aug; Ojai open to all riders Aug 19-20, fared in CA from Aug 14) x the hub-vs-CPUC CA ratio 1.058 [1.040, 1.076] = 11.00M; Maricopa 4.2M (Phoenix freeways + East Valley +55 sq mi Aug 13), Travis 1.90M, Atlanta 1.36M (hub Q2 run-rate 1.08M/mo) (±25%); + uncounted metros 2.3M [1.4M, 3.8M] (Dallas open to all Aug 4, Houston Aug 20). No pause, recall, or halt found for August. No Waymo total-miles figure measured after Jun 2026 exists; later figures are floors: 'more than 4,000' vehicles (TechCrunch, Sep 1; it already said 'around 4,000' on Jun 29; the Jun 13 recall covered 3,871 5th-gen vehicles) and 'more than half a million paid trips weekly' (Waymo, Sep 15, 2026; the same floor Waymo has stated since late March), while '4M+ mi/wk' was last stated Jun 24. Next anchors: CPUC Q3 (~mid-Nov) and the hub update with data thru Sep (~mid/late Dec)."
zoox,2026-08,255000,3265920,2594213,3927673,178500,331500,1,1,1,1,1,1,"US rough est.: paid Las Vegas rides from Aug 10, 2026 (9 free + 22 paid days; ~65 vehicles running against the NTA 100-vehicle cap; hours unpublished until ~Sep 20-25, when zoox.com first stated 8am-1am; fares ~$22 avg vs Lyft ~$14, LVRJ Aug 17) + SF still free/waitlist (CPUC Jun actual 67,923 mi; needs deployment permits to charge) + Austin/Miami employee rides; Uber-app integration not live ('later this year'). No volume disclosure since the Aug 5 'more than 3 million miles'/'more than half a million riders' (Jul 30) statements — the press 'nearly 1 million riders' conflates riders with waitlist. Paid pricing likely trims demand but VMT is supply/deadhead-driven, so ~flat vs July; 0.7x-1.3x band. No pause, recall, or halt found for August. FMVSS exemption FR 2026-15485: ≤2,500 vehicles per 12 months through Jul 31, 2028."
tesla,2026-09,1005000,4241000,4004800,4976000,925000,1235000,0.28,0.2,0.52,0.2876,0.1327,1.0,"Third month on Tesla's no-monitor miles (method in the 2026-07 row). September: 971,738 on the page, 925,284 in the deck (whose end-September total, 1,875,832, Tesla's text calls 'nearly two million'); the 4.8% gap is between Tesla's two publications, not interpolation (a within-week growth curve moves the page's figure by about 8k). Floor 925,000; the central, 1,005,000, adds June's 33,000 monitor-aboard remainder; the top, 1,235,000, keeps the monitor-aboard share at or below June's 21%. Weekly no-monitor miles rose from 171,952 (week to Sep 6) to 258,043 (week to Sep 27), the first full month of Cybercab rides in Austin (from Sep 3, per the Q3 deck). The 480,000 placeholder this replaces sat below the floor."
zoox,2026-09,265000,3530920,2779713,4272173,185500,344500,0.28,0.2,0.52,0.2876,0.1327,1.0,"US rough est.: first full month of paid Las Vegas service (from Aug 10) plus paid LAS airport pickups at both terminals from Sep 3 (Zoox quotes 20-45 minutes per airport trip); ~65 LV vehicles in service (NTA, Sep 2) within a purpose-built fleet of about 100 (TechCrunch Sep 17); the NTA 100-vehicle cap expired Sep 25 (growth 'over the next several months', so October on); SF still free (DMV deployment permit pending); Austin/Miami rides limited to Zoox employees, families and friends (Zoox, Mar 25 and Apr 23, 2026; public waitlists); Uber-app integration not live. No miles or riders disclosure since Aug 5 ('more than 3 million miles'); NYT Sep 9's unattributed 'about 10,000 rides a week' may be riders at the Q2 pace, and as rides would imply ~210-380k mi/mo. August per-day (8.2k) x 30 = 247k, nudged up for the airport: 265k; 0.7x-1.3x band. No pause, recall or halt found for driverless service, including after the Sep 25 Strip crash (no passengers aboard, per Zoox via News 3 LV, Sep 26; NHTSA's Oct 2 recall file lists no new Zoox recall; the Sep 23 Atlanta grounding covers the safety-driver Highlander test fleet, outside this scope)."
waymo,2026-09,21429027,339495163,323985659,357965047,16578600,27143960,0.28,0.2,0.52,0.2876,0.1327,1.0,"US est., bottom-up extension on the hub basis (re-chained 2026-09-25): CA = 10.4M CPUC-basis held (±15%) x the hub-vs-CPUC CA ratio 1.058 [1.040, 1.076] = 11.00M; Maricopa 4.2M (4.3M less ~0.1M for the Phoenix weather pauses below), Travis 1.95M, Atlanta 1.43M (hub Q2 run-rate 1.08M/mo) (±25%); + uncounted metros 2.85M [1.7M, 4.8M] (Denver/San Diego/Tampa public from Sep 1, San Diego counted here and not in the CPUC-basis CA term; Las Vegas public from Sep 14 with 'a couple of dozen vehicles'; San Antonio passenger service back Sep 17 for prior-access riders, 'dozens' of cars). Austin freeway rides via Uber from Sep 22 (a Waymo spokesperson to CBS Austin, Sep 23: 'millions of fully autonomous freeway miles we've already driven with riders in Phoenix, Miami, San Francisco, and Los Angeles'); Nashville riders matched on Lyft from Sep 9; Texas AV registrations 736 (Aug 25) to 988 (Sep 2) to 1,133 (Sep 25) to 1,154 (Oct 1; 359 Zeekr Ojai), registered not the same as in service. Cross-check: 21.4M/mo ≈ 5.0M/wk vs Waymo's last stated floor, 'more than 4 million miles every week' (Jun 24). Weather pauses in robotaxitracker app-availability data (every pickup point unavailable): Phoenix Sep 16 13:28 MST to Sep 17 08:30 MST, during NWS flash flood warnings and ending as the last flood warning expired, then 28-30 of 37 points until ~11:20 MST Sep 18, and Sep 29 04:38-07:18 MST (Polo remnants), about 1 demand-weighted day in all (~0.1M, taken off Maricopa); LA Sep 6 17:19-21:09 PDT (flood advisories); Dallas from Sep 30 16:19 CDT, when a flood watch began, into October. No recall after 26E035000 and no new NHTSA investigation through Sep 30. No Waymo total-miles figure measured after Jun 2026 exists; later figures are floors: 'more than 4,000' vehicles (TechCrunch, Sep 1; it already said 'around 4,000' on Jun 29; the Jun 13 recall covered 3,871 5th-gen vehicles) and 'more than half a million paid trips weekly' (Waymo, Sep 15, 2026; the same floor Waymo has stated since late March), while '4M+ mi/wk' was last stated Jun 24. Next anchors: CPUC Q3 (~mid-Nov) and the hub update with data thru Sep (~mid/late Dec)."
teslanomonitor,2026-01,547,547,531,1110,531,1110,1,1,1,1,1,1,"Tesla's no-monitor line: miles Tesla counts as driven with no safety monitor in the vehicle (remote assistance included), from its Robotaxi Safety Report page (tesla.com/robotaxi/safety, live Oct 21, 2026: weekly cumulative miles per city; a month takes the city sums at its end, interpolated within that week) and, as a second reading, its Q3-2026 deck chart 'Cumulative Unsupervised Robotaxi Miles' (month ends read from the PDF's vector paths). The service began Jan 22, 2026. January: 547 on the page, 1,077 in the deck. Central: the page; band: the two readings widened 3% each way for interpolation, the lower edge at least half the page's figure. Crashes on this line: the in-scope Tesla reports on Tesla's own no-monitor list."
