tesla,2026-10,2594000,6835000,6079800,8611000,2075000,3635000,"October, in progress (method in the 2026-07 row; data/README.md, Ghost months): Tesla's no-monitor miles for the month as projected on the no-monitor line, 2,561,000 [2,075,000, 3,289,000] (1,179,333 measured for Oct 1-18 on Tesla's page, plus a projection of Oct 19-31), set the floor; the central adds June's 33,000 monitor-aboard remainder, unmeasured since June; the top combines the no-monitor line's top and a monitor-aboard share at or below June's 21%, the two uncertainties taken together rather than stacked (for July-September, whose no-monitor miles are measured, this gives their tops within 1.2%). Houston's no-monitor miles fell from 83,976 in July to 21,942 in September and 16,876 over Oct 1-18, and a secondary source (thechargeport, Oct 25) calls Houston's operations mixed; if those cars kept driving with monitors aboard, the remainder is about 55-90k, inside the band."
zoox,2026-10,290000,3820920,2980713,4711173,201000,439000,"US est., October in progress (data/README.md, Ghost months): Zoox's rate between 'nearly two million autonomous miles' (Mar 24, 2026) and 'more than 3 million miles on public roads' (Aug 5), read as 1.88-2.00M and 3.00-3.20M, is 8,657 miles a day: California about 2,476 (CPUC driverless miles Apr-Jun 47,323 / 65,708 / 67,923, plus 19% for the DMV-reported miles CPUC misses) and the rest, mostly Las Vegas, about 6,181, held flat (no fleet count since about 65 vehicles in service on Sep 2). California grown 5% a month from June: 3,291 a day. Total 9,472 a day x 31 = 293,615, rounded to 290,000. Band x0.69 to x1.51: statement rounding, drift over the 4.6 months since the window's midpoint, and month noise, plus an upside term for announced fleet growth (the NTA's 100-vehicle cap expired Sep 25; NHTSA's Oct 6 operating authorization allows 600 vehicles at once; Las Vegas service starts at 6 a.m. from late October). The same model puts August and September near 284k and 279k, above their rows, so part of the step from September is the change of method. Next anchors: Amazon's Q3 call (Oct 29), CPUC Q3 (about mid-November)."
waymo,2026-10,22347000,361842163,340840659,386091047,16855000,28126000,"US est., October in progress (data/README.md, Ghost months): the September row's terms at their per-day rates for 31 days. CA 10.4M CPUC-basis x 31/30 = 10.75M x the hub-vs-CPUC CA ratio 1.0576 = 11.37M; Maricopa 4.44M (September's 4.3M before its weather deduction), Travis 2.02M, Atlanta 1.48M; uncounted metros 3.05M (2.95M at September's rate, plus about 0.15M for Tampa, San Diego and San Antonio opening to anyone on Oct 14 and Nashville airport rides from Oct 13, less about 0.05M for the Oct 1-2 storm pauses in Dallas, Houston and Nashville). Total 22.35M, about 721k a day (September's row 714k; measured Q2 608k). Band x0.754 to x1.259, from a backtest of four-month-ahead extrapolation on CPUC's monthly California miles and the hub's quarterly county miles (October ends four months after Q2, the latest quarter both have measured), the components' ranges combined at a correlation of 0.2 to 0.8. In NHTSA's Oct-15 file Waymo's in-scope crashes fell from 97 a month in Jan-Jun to 87 in July and 66 in August, mostly in California: either fewer crashes per mile or fewer miles than the July-September rows carry. Next anchors: Alphabet's Q3 call (Oct 28), CPUC Q3 (about mid-November), the hub update through September (about mid/late December)."
teslanomonitor,2026-10,2561000,4494209,3889075,5288181,2075000,3289000,"Tesla's no-monitor line (method in the 2026-01 row), October in progress (band rule in data/README.md, Ghost months). Tesla's page as of Oct 18 gives 1,179,333 for Oct 1-18. Oct 19-31 is projected from its last four weeks (258,043 / 334,281 / 430,621 / 557,694 miles, each about 29% above the one before) as the geometric mean of two projections: the mean of holding the last week flat and continuing its growth (1,256,372), and continuing the three-week growth (1,520,118). That is 1,381,968 miles, 21.4% growth a week; holding the last week flat, as the page's live counter does, gives 1,035,717. Central 2,561,000. Band: the projected part x0.66 to x1.51, which in a backtest on the page's own weekly series held every 13-day stretch from late April to early October, plus the 4.8% gap between Tesla's page and its Q3 deck; the low edge allows a 9.5% weekly decline for two weeks, the high edge about 60% weekly growth. Context: Tesla says weekly growth continues (Q3 call, Oct 21); 300+ Cybercabs serving Austin (Tesla, Oct 10); Austin hours to midnight from about Oct 16; Houston's service area grew on Oct 26. Re-derive when the page adds the weeks of Oct 19 and Oct 26."
